   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI_04">Kreuzstrasse 19</name> <hi rend="superscript">III</hi>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI">Zurich</name> VIII: <name type="geo" key="G_CHE">Switzerland</name>
                            </addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <opener>
                        <salute>Dear Miss <name type="person" key="weav76">Weaver</name>
                        </salute>
                    </opener>	<p>I have <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">received</seg> your second remittance of £25 and also your very kind letter and I enclose a regular receipt for the whole amount. I beg leave to thank you once more for your benevolence and to assure you that you have helped me most generously at a most difficult moment. I am writing to <name type="person" key="pink62">Mr Pinker</name> again urging him to conclude the <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">matter</seg> as quickly as possible. I am sorry you had such <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">trouble</seg> about the copies of the <name type="work" key="THE_EGOIST">
                            <hi rend="underline">Egoist</hi>
                        </name> which you sent me and hope that they will now be sent on.</p>        	<p>Many thanks indeed for your good wishes for my birthday. It is many years indeed since I received such a welcome message for that day as your last letter brought me.</p>        	<closer>
                        <salute>With sincerest thanks and kindest regards</salute>
                        <salute>sincerely yours</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed> <dateline>
                            <date when="1916-02-05">5 February 1916</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
                <div type="appendix">
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg4" type="note-anchor">Received</seg> from <hi rend="underline">The</hi> <hi rend="underline">Egoist</hi> <hi rend="underline">Ltd</hi> the sum of £50 (fifty pounds sterling), payment for serial rights (<date when="1914-02-02">2 February 1914</date> – <date when="1914-09-01">1 September 1915</date>) of my novel <name type="work" key="A_PORTRAIT">
                            <hi rend="underline">A</hi> <hi rend="underline">Portrait</hi> <hi rend="underline">of</hi> <hi rend="underline">the</hi> <hi rend="underline">Artist</hi> <hi rend="underline">as</hi> <hi rend="underline">a</hi> <hi rend="underline">Young</hi> <hi rend="underline">Man</hi>
                        </name>
                    </p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>With thanks</salute>
                        <signed>James Joyce</signed> <dateline>Zurich (Switzerland): <date when="1916-02-05">5 February 1916</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">On <date when="1916-01-12">12 January 1916</date>, Ezra Pound had written to Harriet Weaver: “I have just had a desperate letter from Joyce. He has ‘answered 60 newspaper ads.’ etc. etc. NO work. If you haven’t already written to him, can you send him £5. at once, as a loan, as advance payment, as anything?” (British Library, Add. MS 57345, pp. 25, 26). On <date when="1916-01-14">14 January 1916</date>, Harriet Weaver wrote to JJ: “Meantime, as our journal, after stringent economics, is now in a better financial position than it was when your novel was running through it, we think it only fair to make you some payment for the past serial rights, &amp; we could now pay you £50” (Firth 156). JJ acknowledges receipt of the £25 in his <rs type="person" key="L_68277">telegram to Harriet Weaver of <date when="1916-01-22">22 January 1916</date>
                    </rs>.</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">In her letter of 14 January 1916 (Firth 156), Weaver had offered to publish <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name> through the Egoist Ltd if JJ was unable to find another publisher. JJ’s letter to Pinker of 5 February 1916 (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LII</name> 374) asked him to contact Weaver to set up a contract.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">Weaver’s letter of 28 January 1916 (Firth 156-57) noted that due to a change in wartime regulations the British Censor’s Office had returned issues of the December 1915 and January 1916 issues of <name type="work" key="W_THEEGOIST">The Egoist</name> that had been intended for JJ.</note>
                <note xml:id="note4" target="#seg4" type="commentary">The following text appears on a separate sheet of paper and was sent with the letter.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
